# Radio group

A set of native radios sharing one name — pick exactly one of a few
visible options. Basecoat styles each input with the `input` class; a
`fieldset` carries the group semantics and its `legend` names the
group, with no extra wiring.

<%= embed :radio_group, :default %>

```html
<fieldset class="grid gap-3">
  <legend class="label">Site</legend>
  <label class="label">
    <input type="radio" class="input" name="site" value="leeds">
    Leeds
  </label>
  <label class="label">
    <input type="radio" class="input" name="site" value="bradford" checked>
    Bradford
  </label>
  <label class="label">
    <input type="radio" class="input" name="site" value="sheffield">
    Sheffield
  </label>
</fieldset>
```

## When to use

- One choice from ≤5 known options, all worth seeing at once:
  site, priority, gender.
- Anywhere a two- or three-option `select` was about to be used.

## When not to use

- Many options — `select` (known list) or `combobox` (searchable).
- Several independent picks — checkboxes.
- On/off — `checkbox` or `switch`.

## Anatomy and options

A `fieldset` of label-wrapped native radios. `class="input"` does the
styling; everything else is the platform's radio API:

- One `name` shared across the group makes the radios exclusive.
- `value` per option; `checked` on the pre-selected one, if any.
- `disabled` per input — on each one to disable the whole group.
- `legend` names the group for everyone; add `sr-only` when the
  surrounding copy already provides a visible caption.

## Do and don't

- Do order options by likelihood or natural order, not
  alphabetically by accident.
- Do leave every option unchecked when the form genuinely has no
  default — forcing the user to choose is sometimes the point.
- Don't use a radio group for an optional choice without an explicit
  "None" option — once picked, a radio can't be un-picked.
- Don't stack more than ~5 options; the scan cost outgrows the
  benefit of visibility.

## Accessibility and content

- Native radios in a `fieldset`: arrow keys move within the group,
  Tab moves past it, the `legend` is announced with each option —
  platform behaviour, no JS and no `role`/`aria` to add.
- Option labels are short noun phrases, parallel in form ("Royal
  Children's", "Monash", not a mix of names and sentences).
